From: Soe Thura <6492082+thixpin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:40:06 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Emit fences for sync, lwsync and eieio

The recompiler drops the memory barriers, which is only safe on strongly ordered hosts (x86).
Forza Motorsport 4 has 64 sync, 120 lwsync and 20 eieio; on ARM64 the hardware and the compiler
may reorder accesses across them. sync becomes a sequentially consistent fence, lwsync (all
orderings but store-load) an acquire-release fence, and eieio (store ordering) a release fence.
---
 XenonRecomp/recompiler.cpp | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/XenonRecomp/recompiler.cpp b/XenonRecomp/recompiler.cpp
index 1688fb1..933ac5a 100644
--- a/XenonRecomp/recompiler.cpp
+++ b/XenonRecomp/recompiler.cpp
@@ -1004,7 +1004,8 @@ bool Recompiler::Recompile(
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_EIEIO:
-        // no op
+        // Orders stores to cacheable memory (store-store); a release fence also orders earlier loads.
+        println("\t__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_RELEASE);");
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_EXTSB:
@@ -1374,7 +1375,8 @@ bool Recompiler::Recompile(
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_LWSYNC:
-        // no op
+        // Orders load-load, load-store and store-store, not store-load: an acquire-release fence.
+        println("\t__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);");
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_LWZ:
@@ -1952,7 +1954,8 @@ bool Recompiler::Recompile(
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_SYNC:
-        // no op
+        // Orders every earlier storage access before every later one.
+        println("\t__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);");
         break;
 
     case PPC_INST_TDLGEI:
